<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 5<br>Part 2 (§§ 9–15 / Oaseyos ט–טו)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:31</span> (<i>Ais Lan Bira B'Davra</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 9 — Korach Swallowed Alive: Separating Letters from Vowel-Points = Death</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Korach and his assembly were swallowed alive, as it says: <span class="v">"they descended alive to the pit."</span> The essential death derives from the above-mentioned damage — by not forming the letters of the Torah for good, which is the aspect of <span class="mx">"if he did not merit, it becomes a potion of death."</span> This damage derives essentially from not drawing close to the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> — all the more when one disputes him, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, and separates <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span>: separating Torah-study from good longings (= good souls, good vowel-points), as above. Through this, <span class="mx">"if he did not merit, it becomes a potion of death"</span> — since he separates the letters of Torah from the vowel-points, which is literally the departure of the soul from the body: the essential death, as above.</div>

<div class="p">The essential death derives from the sin of Adam HaRishon through the sin of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. The essential damage of the Tree of Knowledge was this very aspect. The Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil is the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span>, encompassing the six orders of Mishnah — the aspect of forbidden and permitted, etc. — as known in the Tikunim. Hashem commanded him not to eat from the Tree of Knowledge at all — He wanted him to cleave only to the Tree of Life, which is entirely good, and then no sorting would have been needed at all. But he sinned and ate from the Tree of Knowledge through the counsel of the serpent, who was more cunning than all, enticing him to become clever: <span class="v">"your eyes will be opened"</span> etc. — enticing him to pursue only wisdoms and investigations, to know good and evil, without striving after good deeds at all. Until the serpent overpowered him and enticed him to violate Hashem's command, to eat from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. He also damaged the holy <span class="h">bris</span>, as our Sages say.</div>

<div class="p">Through all this he was in the aspect of <span class="mx">"a gossip separates a chief"</span> — he separated <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span>, like <span class="h">Acher</span> who "cut the plantings," as above. For through eating from the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil and through damaging the <span class="h">bris</span>, he caused a separation between the aspect of <span class="h">lamdan</span> (= the six orders of Mishnah, <span class="h">Metat</span>-angel) and the aspect of <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= guarding the <span class="h">bris</span>, <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span>), as above. This is the separation of the letters from the vowel-points — the aspect of body and soul. Therefore death was decreed through this — the departure of the soul from the body — for he damaged by separating the vowel-points from the letters, which is literally the departure of the soul from the body, as above.</div>

<div class="p">But Adam HaRishon did <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and began to rectify somewhat. Likewise all the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation work on this <span class="h">tikun</span>. The essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who overcame their inclination until they merited <span class="h">tikun habris</span> in truth — through which they merited good longings and making good vowel-points for the letters, through which everything is formed for good: the aspect of life, the aspect of <span class="mx">"if he merited, it becomes a potion of life,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">Whoever wants to protect his life must bind himself to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, for with them is the source of life, as above. In every generation they work to rectify the sin of Adam HaRishon through the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and those who draw close to them — through which <span class="h">lamdan</span> and <span class="h">tzadik</span> are included together, vowel-points and letters, Torah and good deeds, the encompassing of body within soul: the aspect of life, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Nevertheless, since this <span class="h">tikun</span> cannot be completed all at once or in a single generation, the decree of death remains in place and ultimately a person must die. Due to the enormity of the damage of Adam HaRishon's sin, the contamination can only be sorted through the departure of the soul from the body. But in truth, now death is a great good, as it says: <span class="mx">"and behold, it was very good — this is death,"</span> as our Sages say. For now, after the sin, even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> cannot return to the source of life in completeness except through the departure of the soul from the body — when <span class="v">"the dust returns to the earth as it was, and the spirit returns to G-d who gave it."</span> Then the contamination of the body decomposes in the dust until, at the time of the Resurrection, the soul and the purified body will reunite and arise in the Resurrection, living true life — eternal life — each according to the good deeds he merited accumulating in this world, and according to his closeness and attachment to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and <span class="h">yir'aim</span>, through which he worked to unite soul and body, as above. Through this he will ultimately merit this union in completeness — eternal life, as above.</div>

<div class="p">But it is impossible to reach eternal life except through death specifically — so the contamination of Adam HaRishon's sin through the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil decomposes. Through this he made a separation between letters and vowel-points — body and soul, as above. Therefore there must be a separation between them (= death) before they merit a true union (= eternal life), as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Korach, who damaged this greatly — disputing Moshe, the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the totality of Torah, wanting to separate <span class="h">lamdan</span> from <span class="h">tzadik</span>, as above — as if it were possible to draw life without the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, as if the letters (= the body) have vitality without the vowel-points, without the true soul. In truth there is no vitality for the body except through the soul, and the soul receives vitality from Above through the vowel-points (= good souls made through good longings that form the letters of Torah for good) — specifically from there vitality flows from the Source of Life to the body and to all the worlds. But one who separates them through the above damage — his life is no life at all, since it does not flow from the Source of Life. Therefore <span class="mx">"the wicked in their lifetime are called dead."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore Korach, who damaged this — as if there is life without the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, brazenly defying Moshe Rabbainu through whom the essential drawing of vitality from the Source of Life to all souls to vitalize the bodies is accomplished — received what he wanted. He and his assembly descended alive to the pit — <span class="v">"alive"</span> specifically — and did not merit death and burial. They gave him what he wanted: he wanted to receive vitality by himself without binding to Moshe; on the contrary, he disputed him greatly. Therefore his vitality was severed from the Source of Life and he remained with all his vitality below. Immediately the earth opened its mouth and swallowed them alive to the pit — for that is his place, to live with such life. The earth (= dust) is in the aspect of letters relative to vowel-points, body relative to soul.</div>

<div class="p">It is known that the four elements — fire, wind, water, earth — correspond to <span class="h">te'amim</span> [cantillation], <span class="h">nekudos</span> [vowel-points], <span class="h">tagin</span> [crowns], <span class="h">osiyos</span> [letters]. Thus earth corresponds to the aspect of letters — which have no vitality except through the vowel-points that receive vitality via the <span class="h">te'amim</span> from the Source of Life and channel vitality to the letters via the <span class="h">tagin</span>. All these aspects are linked one to another, receiving one from the other, ascending higher and higher, as is known. Likewise all creatures of the world — all created by Hashem from dust, as it says: <span class="v">"everything was from the dust"</span> — all receive one from another, ascending ever higher. Since Korach and his assembly made such a separation and wanted to live by themselves without the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= Moshe), they remained below with their life, which is in the aspect of dust, as above. Therefore they descended specifically alive to the pit — since they separated their vitality from the Source of Life by disputing the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is Moshe, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 10 — Pinchas: Zealotry for the <i>Bris</i> = Eternal Life</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"Pinchas son of Elazar son of Aharon the Kohain turned back My wrath... therefore say... it shall be for him and his seed after him a covenant of eternal priesthood"</span> etc. Through Pinchas's zealotry for the <span class="h">bris</span>, he merited the <span class="h">kehunah</span>. For the <span class="h">kehunah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">chesed</span>, love, yearning, and good longings for Hashem (= the aspect of Avraham, man of <span class="h">chesed</span>) — is merited through <span class="h">tikun habris</span>, as explained well in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Therefore the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> is called <span class="h">Kohain</span>: <span class="v">"the lips of the Kohain shall guard knowledge, and Torah shall be sought from his mouth"</span> — from which our Sages expound: <span class="mx">"if the teacher resembles an angel of Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> etc. This encompasses the entire above discourse: <span class="v">"angel of Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> is the aspect of the totality and union of <span class="h">lamdan</span> and <span class="h">tzadik</span> — letters and vowel-points — as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Pinchas, who is Eliyahu, merited eternal life in his lifetime — he ascended to heaven and lives forever. He is the exact opposite of the assembly of Korach, who disputed Moshe: they remained with their life far below in the lowest pit, having separated their vitality from the Source of Life through disputing the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, as above. But Pinchas, who fulfilled Moshe's will completely and was zealous for the <span class="h">bris</span> until he merited the <span class="h">kehunah</span> — the above <span class="h">chesed</span> in completeness — good longings that are good souls, the essential true vitality, as above — he merited binding himself to the Source of Life with such completeness that he remained alive and enduring forever. Through his zealotry for <span class="v">"Hashem Tz'vakos"</span> and fulfilling Moshe's will and giving his life to death for this — through this the contamination of the serpent (from the damage of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil) departed from him completely, as if he had endured the suffering of death itself and returned alive in the Resurrection of the Dead.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential <span class="h">techiyas hamasim</span> [Resurrection] is through him, as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"the Resurrection of the Dead comes through Eliyahu, remembered for good."</span> The essential Resurrection — eternal life — is merited by including the letters within the vowel-points, through binding and attachment to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are the aspect of Moshe, who merited <span class="h">tikun habris</span> in completeness. Pinchas, who is Eliyahu, merited all this in his lifetime — therefore he merited true life and lives forever, and all Resurrection will be through him, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 11 — Pinchas's Battle Through Prayer and Forming Letters for Good</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Pinchas's essential victory was through <span class="h">tefilah</span>, as it says: <span class="v">"Pinchas stood and prayed"</span> etc. Pinchas's essential battle was against Bilam the wicked, by whose counsel this entire evil affair occurred, as it says: <span class="v">"behold, these caused [sin] by the word of Bilam."</span> For Balak took counsel to send for Bilam because his power was in his mouth, as our Sages say: he said, "Moshe's power is only in his mouth — I too will bring against them a man whose power is in his mouth." All this is the above matter — the great power of good longings, etc., as above; and conversely, the great evil caused by evil longings, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Moshe Rabbainu — his power was only in his mouth. He merited more than anyone in the world to form the letters of Torah for good — because he was holy in the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> with the ultimate holiness, as our Sages say. Therefore he merited good longings and making good souls and vowel-points for all the letters of the holy Torah — until he merited pairing, combining, and forming all the letters of Torah for good. Therefore he alone merited giving the Torah to Israel — what no <span class="h">tzadik</span> before him had merited. Only he, through his awesome holiness, merited forming all the letters of Torah for good — until it was combined in these holy combinations in a way that he could draw it forth and give it to us, to merit us forever.</div>

<div class="p">For it is known that the Torah already existed — the Torah preceded the world. But Hashem did not want to give it to human beings until there would be a <span class="h">tzadik</span> who would combine and form it, through his holiness, in good combinations befitting to give to those below. Therefore the world was collapsing until Moshe came and received the Torah — he merited it by combining and unifying the letters of Torah for good through his holiness, as above.</div>

<div class="p">The beginning was from Avraham Avinu, who was the man of <span class="h">chesed</span>, as brought in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Avraham merited the aspect of guarding <span class="h">bris ilah</span>, through which he merited <span class="h">chesed</span> — good longings — through which he made souls. Therefore he began to combine and form the letters of Torah for good, and merited Torah: <span class="v">"because Avraham hearkened to My voice and guarded My safeguards, My commandments, My statutes, and My teachings."</span> As our Sages say: <span class="mx">"Avraham Avinu fulfilled even <i>airuvai tavshilin</i>."</span> But even though his holiness enabled forming it for good, he did not merit forming it in such completeness that it would be fit to be given to those below. Likewise Yitzchak, Yaakov, and the Tribes — all worked on this and rectified much, but did not complete this <span class="h">tikun</span> in completeness.</div>

<div class="p">Until Moshe Rabbainu came, who was holy with such awesome holiness that he merited such good longings and holy desires — for Moshe ascended to the Will within Wills, through which he merited ascending there, as stated in the holy Zohar. Therefore he merited combining and forming it for good with such completeness that it was fit to be given to us — so the entire world would be governed and sustained for all generations through it.</div>

<div class="p">Because of this, his power was in his mouth — to govern the entire world as he wished and perform exceedingly great and awesome signs and wonders in the world, which had never been heard or seen. All his power was in his mouth. Since he merited forming all the letters of Torah for good in the utmost completeness, he certainly had the power to perform awesome wonders through the speech of his holy mouth — combining the letters of Torah and speaking holy words through his mouth, through which he performed awesome wonders, governing the world as he wished: water becoming dry land, etc. The entire world is governed through the letters of Torah, according to how one merits combining them, as above and as explained there at the end of the above <span class="h">Torah</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"in His goodness He renews"</span> etc.; <span class="mx">"there is no 'good' but Torah."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore Balak took counsel to bring against them Bilam, whose power was also in his mouth. Bilam was in the <span class="h">klipah</span> corresponding to Moshe, as our Sages say. Through his great impurity and exceedingly evil longings, he could combine the letters for the reverse, as above — for <span class="v">"G-d made one corresponding to the other."</span> Therefore Israel was truly in great danger then, as it says: <span class="v">"My people, remember what Balak counseled"</span> etc. But Hashem in His mercy had compassion on Israel and gave power to the good longings of Moshe and Israel to overpower the evil longings and vapors of Bilam the wicked — until He twisted his mouth, and he was compelled to combine the letters for good, to bless Israel.</div>

<div class="p">This matter occurs in every generation, as I understood from Rabbainu's words and holy hints <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span>. Regarding the vapors that emerge through good or evil longings, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, there are many hidden things, as explained somewhat in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> on the verse <span class="v">"there is a vanity done on the earth"</span> — the aspect of the transmigration of souls; see there, and as brought above. Hashem has compassion on His people in every generation and performs great wonders alone, <span class="v">"for His kindness endures forever"</span> — overpowering at every moment the holy vapors and longings (= <span class="h">chesed</span>, as above) over the evil longings and vapors, so they do not destroy the world, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Hashem commanded remembering the affair of Bilam: <span class="v">"My people, remember what [Balak] counseled... and what Bilam answered"</span> etc. — to know that even now Hashem does for us as He did then, thwarting the counsel of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> at every turn. In His love and compassion, He sends us in every generation such true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> whose holiness has the power to overpower the holy vapors (formed from good longings) over the vapors of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> (formed from evil longings).</div>

<div class="p">The essential battle — Pinchas won through the power of Moshe to fight this war against the counsel of Bilam the wicked. Through his holiness he overpowered the holy vapors and formed the letters of Torah for good. Pinchas's essential victory was through forming the letters for good, as stated in the holy Zohar on the verse <span class="v">"he took a spear in his hand"</span>: he saw the <span class="h">mem</span> etc. which was drifting and about to combine with <span class="h">vav-sav</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — nearly causing destruction, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. He, with his power, took the <span class="h">mem</span> and combined it with the letters of his name, which equal <span class="h">rach</span> [208], and through this <span class="h">r'mach</span> [248 = spear] was formed, and with this he overpowered them. He also engaged in <span class="h">tefilah</span>: <span class="v">"Pinchas stood and prayed."</span> It was an exceedingly intense battle, requiring many miracles, as our Sages say. His entire battle was through combining letters for good, as above. Therefore he merited an eternal <span class="h">kehunah</span> — the aspect of the above love and <span class="h">chesed</span>: <span class="v">"the lips of the Kohain shall guard knowledge"</span> etc., as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 12 — The Census After Pinchas: 600,000 Letters of Torah = 600,000 Souls</div>

<div class="p">This is why the census of Israel is placed adjacent to this, adding to each Jew a <span class="h">heh</span> and <span class="h">yud</span> from His Name — to say: <span class="mx">"My Name testifies for them,"</span> as it says: <span class="v">"the tribes of G-d, testimony for Israel"</span> etc. For the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, in his great holiness of <span class="h">bris kodesh</span>, draws the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> upon every Jew — until all who bind themselves to him are included in the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"your people are all righteous"</span> etc. For every Jew has the aspect of the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> through being circumcised, as I heard from him <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span>. Also, generally there are still many good points of <span class="h">shemiras habris</span> found in every Jew, as our Sages say on the verse: <span class="v">"fenced with roses."</span></div>

<div class="p">But one's own small holiness does not have the power to overpower what needs overpowering — only through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who is holy in the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> with the ultimate holiness in a completeness beyond which there is none. Whoever merits not disputing him — all the more if he merits drawing close to him and being called by his name — through the power of his holiness he too merits good longings and making souls (= good vowel-points), and overpowering them against the evil souls, and forming the letters of Torah for good — by striving to express the good longings through his mouth, through which he makes good vowel-points for the letters and forms them for good, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="mx">"My Name testifies for them."</span> The name is the soul and vitality, as it says: <span class="v">"a living soul — that is its name."</span> Therefore Hashem combined the letters <span class="h">yud-keh</span> from His Name into the names of Israel — to demonstrate that the name (= the soul) of Israel is included in His holy Name. This is certainly great testimony that they are among the guardians of the <span class="h">bris</span>, through which specifically one merits good souls — the aspect of a holy name in which His Name is partnered. This is: <span class="v">"the tribes of G-d, testimony for Israel, to give thanks to the Name of Hashem"</span> — <span class="v">"to give thanks"</span> specifically: the aspect of <span class="h">tefilah</span> and thanksgiving. The essential testimony is <span class="h">tefilah</span> and thanksgiving — what Israel merits, being called by the name of Moshe (= the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>): they merit increasing <span class="h">tefilah</span> and thanksgiving, formed from good combinations through good longings, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of His commanding to count them then. For the 600,000 souls of Israel correspond to the 600,000 letters of Torah. Therefore, after the deed of Pinchas who sanctified the Name in Shittim, He commanded to count them — to demonstrate that the righteousness of the generation's <span class="h">tzadikim</span> prevailed, through the power of Moshe Rabbainu, until all the 600,000 letters of Torah were formed for good, and they are all in their completeness and fullness. This itself is the aspect of the number of the children of Israel — 600,000 in completeness, not one lacking. For His Name testifies for them — they are all rooted and included in the letters of Torah that were all formed for good through Pinchas in the power of Moshe, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 13 — Dividing <i>Eretz Yisrael</i>: Each One's Portion According to His Holiness</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"to these the Land shall be divided according to the number of names."</span> For <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>, as it says: <span class="v">"dwell in the Land and tend emunah,"</span> as our master, teacher, and rabbi <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes many times. Through the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span> — through which the letters are formed for good — one merits <span class="h">emunah</span>, as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. Therefore: <span class="v">"to these the Land shall be divided"</span> — each person according to how much he merits the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span>, proportionally he has a portion in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — Moshe's Departure and the Appointment of Yehoshua: <i>Bris Tata'ah</i> Included in <i>Bris Ilah</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore, adjacent to this comes the departure of Moshe and his request from Hashem to appoint a man over the congregation — <span class="v">"let not the congregation of Hashem be like sheep without a shepherd"</span> etc. — and Hashem's response to ordain Yehoshua. For Moshe saw that to conquer <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> (= to merit Israel complete <span class="h">emunah</span>), there is no one with the power for this like himself — for he was in the aspect of <span class="h">bris ilah</span>, exceedingly holy in the holiness of the <span class="h">bris</span>, as above, through which one merits <span class="h">emunah</span> (= <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>), as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore he thought: perhaps the vow can be annulled, and he himself would bring Israel into the Land — since he sees each time who stands against Israel to destroy them, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, as he saw in the affair of Balak and Bilam. Therefore it was fitting that he bring Israel into the Land, for only he knows how to fight all who stand against them physically and spiritually. But Hashem answered that the decree had already been sealed and cannot be annulled — he would depart here.</div>

<div class="p">Then he asked Hashem: if so, what will Israel do? As it says: <span class="v">"let not the congregation of Hashem be like sheep without a shepherd."</span> Hashem answered: ordain Yehoshua, his student. Yehoshua is the aspect of <span class="h">na'ar</span> [youth] — the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span>, <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span>, as it says: <span class="v">"Yehoshua bin Nun was a na'ar"</span> etc. But because he merited serving Moshe so greatly, as it says: <span class="v">"he did not depart from within the tent"</span> — he merited including <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span> within <span class="h">bris ilah</span>: a <span class="h">lamdan</span> included within a <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the essential completeness. Therefore he has the power to lead Israel — through Moshe's laying his hands upon him.</div>

<div class="p">He merited through his great service being included in Moshe — including <span class="h">tzadik</span> within <span class="h">lamdan</span> — through which the letters are formed for good, through which the entire world is governed for good, through which Israel can be led and <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> conquered (= <span class="h">emunah</span>), merited through the inclusion of <span class="h">bris tata'ah</span> within <span class="h">bris ilah</span> (= the binding and inclusion of <span class="h">lamdan</span> within <span class="h">tzadik</span>), as understood in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Moshe said: <span class="v">"Let Hashem, G-d of the spirits, appoint"</span> etc. Hashem answered: <span class="v">"Take Yehoshua bin Nun, a man in whom there is spirit."</span> <span class="v">"Spirits"</span> specifically; <span class="v">"in whom there is spirit"</span> specifically. Everything depends on the spirit and vapor — the soul and breath of life — drawn according to the longings, as above. Moshe requested from Hashem: <span class="v">"Let Hashem, G-d of the spirits, appoint a man"</span> etc. — that he should have the power to overpower the soul and holy breath of life (= the holy vapors) over the spirit and vapors of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>. Hashem answered: take Yehoshua, <span class="v">"a man in whom there is spirit"</span> — <span class="mx">"who knows how to go against the spirit of each and every one"</span> — who knows how to overpower the breath of life of each person, strengthening the spirit and holy vapor formed from good longings over the spirit and vapor of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> formed from evil longings. Through this the essential governance of the world for good is accomplished — through which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is conquered (= <span class="h">emunah</span>), the completeness of everything, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — The <i>Korbanos</i>: <i>Tzedakah</i> Governing the Spheres</div>

<div class="p">Through <span class="h">emunah</span>, the <span class="h">tzedakah</span> that governs the celestial spheres is completed, as above. This is why adjacent to this come all the <span class="h">parshiyos</span> of the <span class="h">korbanos</span> for every day, <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span>, and <span class="h">Yomim Tovim</span>. All the <span class="h">korbanos</span> are voluntary offerings of the heart — the aspect of <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — through which all the celestial spheres are governed.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it is a <span class="h">mitzvah</span> to offer the <span class="h">temidim</span> [daily offerings] every day, morning and evening — at the time of sunrise and sunset, as it says: <span class="v">"two per day."</span> It is a <span class="h">mitzvah</span> to slaughter them corresponding to the day, corresponding to the sunrise and sunset, as our Sages say — to demonstrate that the entire governance of the sun (the greatest of all the spheres, through which the essential order of the seasons is maintained) is entirely through the <span class="h">korbanos</span> of Israel, which are the aspect of <span class="h">tzedakah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">He also commanded to add <span class="h">musafim</span> [additional offerings] on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, and <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span>. For all the changes of seasons are through the spheres — through the seven planets corresponding to the seven days of the week, and through the twelve constellations corresponding to the twelve months in which all the festivals fall, as it says: <span class="v">"let them be for signs and for festivals, for days and years."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore <span class="h">musafim</span> must be offered on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, and <span class="h">Rosh Chodesh</span> — to govern for good all the celestial spheres in all the changes of the seasons, all of which are governed through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> (= the <span class="h">korbanos</span>), as above. Thus all the <span class="h">parshiyos</span> of the <span class="h">sedra</span> are linked and adjacent to one another beautifully — <span class="v">"supported forever, formed in truth and uprightness."</span></div>

